The important boundary is **when** Moth code runs. A folded template produces an
ordinary String. It owns no DOM element, listener or rerender job.

### Synchronous DOM work

A small `@web/dom` API should expose browser primitives, not
`sync_docs_sidebar()`-style application helpers. The docs package can then
implement navigation synchronisation and geometry calculations in ordinary
Moth.

The current sidebar splits its work across two JavaScript paths.
`@web/page`'s `sync_navigation()` marks active links and builds breadcrumbs,
while `sidebar.js` measures the navbar, aligns the current category heading and
schedules updates on scroll and resize. A full migration moves those algorithms
into Moth and leaves only generic host bindings in JavaScript.

DOM operations must distinguish normal absence from a failed operation. "No
previous sibling" is an optional result. A malformed selector is an error. A
`require_by_id()`-style source helper could turn a missing required element into
`Error!` while the low-level lookup keeps serving optional elements.

DOM element handles represent browser-owned identity. A Moth record containing a
handle neither owns nor extends the element's DOM lifetime. Mutation follows the
handle's explicit access contract, and source ownership, borrow validation and
lifetime topology still apply. The foreign runtime must define explicit teardown
rules for retained resources.

The first binding should stay deliberately small: ID and selector lookup,
attributes and text, checkbox state, classes, element creation and child
mutation, bounding boxes, computed CSS and scroll position. Prefer one cohesive
operation when repeated scalar calls would cause excessive host crossings. The
DOM plan must settle the exact opaque, optional and result ABI shapes, so don't
guess them in a user example.

### Typed event delivery

The intended data flow:

[codeblock, $code "text":
    browser scroll or resize
      -> host listener and coalescing
      -> construct an ordinary Moth message
      -> Channel writer
      -> scheduled Moth consumer
      -> @web/dom update
]

A message domain can use an ordinary choice:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- Illustrative future event domain

    SidebarEvent ::
        Scrolled,
        Resized,
        Expanded | open Bool |,
    ;
]

The accepted Channel direction provides separate reader and writer endpoints:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- Accepted form, deferred runtime

    reader, writer Channel of SidebarEvent = Channel()
]

A `Route of SidebarEvent` or `Route of Bool -> SidebarEvent` is a **function
parameter contract** for completing a known choice constructor. It holds no
Channel destination, cannot be stored as a normal component field and never
runs an arbitrary callback. A `Wire of T` preserves a named binding identity,
not an event observer.

A host subscription will need an explicit writer destination, a validated
message constructor route and an owned subscription lifetime. It also needs
host-to-Moth re-entry, task scheduling, cancellation and safe retained capture.
The final user-facing subscription signature is not designed yet.

### Scheduling and failure

For high-frequency scroll events, request at most one geometry update per
browser animation frame. Share that scheduling primitive with the future
`@web/graphics.frame` rather than duplicating it inside DOM and Events. Raw
events should not force a layout read for every browser notification.

Page startup handles its own `Error!` outcome. Later event-handler errors happen
**after** startup and need an independent policy for reporting or recovery.
Closing a Channel or removing a component must cancel its listeners. JavaScript
garbage collection cannot decide when a Moth subscription ends.

Use ordinary functions and explicit activation for predictable lifecycle
ownership. A future interactive application can combine typed choices, Channels,
DOM operations and state without closures or an implicit rerendering framework.

The first implementation milestone is synchronous `@web/dom`. Typed
`@web/events` waits for the Channel, async and host-ingress contracts. HTML-JS
can support the first package before other target backends, with unsupported
reachable calls diagnosed by the compiler.

See @../../memory (Memory and lifetimes), @../../errors (Errors) and
@../../packages/builder/graphics/ (@web/graphics).
